			<div class="content" id="post-content-4855347">
									

<p>Hello Blackvvwater! lets take a look :)</p>
<p><strong>SAND</strong></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><strong>The concept:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Elevator Pitch: A young orphan boy is found in possession of a quilt with anomalous properties. He is also being stalked by anomalous entities who wish to take the blanket from him.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My main issue, is there doesn't seem to be much of a connection between the quilt and the entities attached to it. Which as a result, makes the concept feel disjointed and hard to get invested in. Along with this, there isn't much context to why or how this is happening, which makes the skip feel close to a &quot;thing that does a thing&quot;.- why should I as the reader care about the concept? what makes this idea stand out for similar premises? what give this an edge?</p>
<p>Another major issue I have is the blankets anomaly; there doesn't seem to be any limit to what it can do, it can literally get whoever posses it out of any danger whatsoever. As result there's no real conflict or attention grabbing elements, as any possible threats are immediately stopped with no time to really develop.- I would highly recommend you either have the blanket with one or two anomalies or put some sort of limit/restriction on how it operates.</p>
<p><strong>The narrative:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Central Narrative: An MTF team is sent to a small farmhouse where anomalous events have been reported. During their sweep the team a young boy appears out of nowhere, clutching a quilt with strange designs. The MTF team convince the boy to accompany them back to a site, where the boy is interviewed by SCP personnel.</p>
<p>The boy reveals that his grandmother, who recently died, gave him the quilt, explaining that it would protect him. Study of the quilt reveals that the designs on it are thaumaturgical symbols of protection, and while in the boy's possession, it displays a number of functions. When he hides under it, the boy cannot be perceived. When wrapped around him, the quilt protects him from physical harm. It can also slowly heal wounds. When he ties it around his neck like a cape, it allows the boy to fly. What's more, while the boy can willingly surrender the quilt, if it's forcibly taken, it returns to him in a matter of moments.</p>
<p>During his detainment, the boy also explains that the quilt protects him from &quot;the bad things.&quot; These &quot;bad things&quot; are anomalous entities that manage to penetrate the site and seem to exhibit ghost-like abilities. SCP personnel discover that they're drawn to the blanket rather than the boy, as the child exhibits no anomalous qualities. The &quot;bad things&quot; also appear blind to the quilt when it's inert, so it's simply a matter of encouraging the child to surrender the quilt. However, SCP personnel find it difficult to convince him to give up the last thing connecting him to his family.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Currently it doesn't feel like there's any building tension, nothing is really leading to a payoff or big reveal, which makes the story feel rather anticlimactic. None of the questions that the concept raised are answered here either, as I'm still unsure on what these entities are, why the skip has the blanket to begin with, what the entities motives are and what the the Foundations motives are.</p>
<p>Overall, the base ideas shown in the concept aren't expanded upon much, which makes the story feel anticlimactic. The storyline also feels a bit cliched and flat as it doesn't give much other than &quot;Foundation finds something, then tests it&quot;.- what will make this narrative interesting? what about it will make the audience invested? who are the characters (this can include the skip itself)? how will they make me as the reader feel?</p>
<p>This is what I'd recommend;</p>
<p>-'Security blankets' are not a new concept, they have likely been done here before, and if they aren't given enough care, they could end up falling into a cliche; you will need to make sure that this puts a unique spin on the premise, try looking through similar concepts in other articles (or even offsite), as they can help with inspiration and comparison, and they allow for you to study how others may have pulled off a similar idea.</p>
<p>- Cut out the entities or give them a strong motive to be after the blanket; currently they feel like a device to create tension as they don't seem connected to the concept, and thy don't play much of a part in the narrative, they are one of the only threats to the blanket. From what I can see, not much (if anything) would need to change if they were removed.</p>
<p>- Nerf the blankets anomalies, and give it more context; this thing is far too powerful, and as a result any possible conflicts are quelled before they can really start. Along with this the blankets origins aren't really given much of an explanation, it doesn't seem to have any real reason to exist, other than to protect the boy (which on its own won't really stand), there needs <em>to</em> be a reason to protect to boy.</p>
<p>That said, if you want any additional feedback (for this or any concept), feel welcome to send a PM. (response times generally range from 2-12 hrs). If your looking for additional feedback, then I would highly recommend the butterfly squad :)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Cheers,</p>
<p>RED</p>

