<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I can certainly see this as a successful SCP, and the concept/narrative of the blanket being the only thing the boy has connecting him to his family is quite intriguing, and potentially emotional. That being said, there are a few places that your story could be tweaked a bit. A lot of them are similar to Red's, and I'd advising re-reading his post to make sure you improve your concept as much as possible.</p>
<ul>
<li>Omnipotence:The blanket does seem to have too much power. For one thing, I'm thinking having the blanket be able to make the boy invisible, invincible, and capable of returning to him a bit much. I'd say scrap one or all of these traits, since they don't seem to be adding much to the story.</li>
<li>Mystery: Why are the creatures chasing the blanket? I get the hole mystery of the unknown thing, but I think your story would be a bit more impactful if you gave a reason for why that was happening, and possibly tied it into the backstory of the boy's family</li>
<li>Narrative: The anomalous properties of the SCP are important to your article, but the story contained in it is the most important part. Make sure you focus on the emotions, characters, and themes behind your story.</li>
<li>Greenlights: From what I can see, you're a first time author. If that's true, I'd say &quot;yes&quot; for seeking greenlights, since you need two to get your concept afloat. I'd definitely be willing to do so if you fixed a couple of things up!</li>
</ul>
<p>Please keep in mind that this critique isn't meant to bash on you or your writing. It's a great core concept, as far as I'm concerned, and I can definitely see it becoming a popular SCP. It just needs a few tweaks and you're good to go! Please do reach out to me and any other butterfly squad members. After all, we're here to help!</p>
