																		Re: SCP Idea - Security Blanket
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/redesert" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6380468); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6380468&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609878558" alt="REDESERT" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6380468)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/redesert" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6380468); return false;" >REDESERT</a></span> <span class="odate time_1609725173 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">04 Jan 2021 01:52</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4861549">
									

<p>Ported from PM conversation:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>So, I was thinking on the security blanket idea, and I'm thinking of going in an alternate idea I had earlier but hadn't fleshed out. What I'm thinking is that the blanket can be activated with a password (like that old game about kids not letting you into the treehouse without a password), and once activated, the user can burrow under the blanket, which takes them into a fantasy world. The world is a relatively small play-world, with a large castle in the center, and protected on four sides by &quot;guardians,&quot; huge mythical creatures that CAN be killed (the grandmother who designed the blanket wanted her grandson to be able to play hero if he wanted to), but will respawn after about an hour or so. There are a few towns in the world, and the inhabitants are all creepily helpful and friendly. The castle itself can be protected by a number of dangerous traps, making it a perfect source of refuge for the inhabitant.</p>
<p>The underlying narrative still hearkens back to the boy refusing to accept his grandmother's death, but this angle has him turn his play world into a darker source of refuge. The solution still involves coaxing him out rather than taking him by force, as the blanket is impervious to most kinds of harm, since it was also meant to protect. In this way, however, it DOES have weaknesses, namely the password allowing anyone to get in if they know it, and while retrieval is still difficult, it's not impossible. I'm also toying with the play world having rules, such as no guns, though the retrieval teams CAN find weapons inside that work in the play world. They just have to obey the rules the grandmother set up for the blanket.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Overall, I feel this would still need to be much more fleshed out, considering there still doesn't seem to be any clear goals from the Foundation and the blanket (while having an origin/purpose), doesn't have any clear reason to be protecting this child in particular.</p>
<p>Along with this, I feel this still has too many effects. There's still the invincible blanket, but there's now more effects being added onto that- there's no clear reason reason why these effects are here and the weaknesses are not specified, which (at least to me) makes the skip as a whole feel OP, and close to a &quot;thing that does a thing&quot;.- try to only keep one or two major effects, also try to make sure relate to its purpose.</p>
<p>As for your narrative, there doesn't seem to be any clear end goal, why is the Foundation so interested in this blanket? why are they sending retrieval teams in? how do they find out about the gun rule? what are the other rules? how does everything end?-currently, the narrative pitch just sort of ends?</p>
<p>Another issue I'm noticing is the emotional route you want this to take. Currently, it feels sort of forced as there's nothing really showing the child and how he/she might feel about everything. What will make me invested in them? how do they affect the narrative? what ends up happening to them? how should make me as the reader feel?</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>So, first, I'm not sure why you're so opposed to the blanket doing what it's supposed to do - protecting a child. If the SCP took extreme measures, certainly, they could probably find a way to destroy the blanket, but that's not what the SCP does - their mission is to contain first, and destroy only if they have no other option. Besides, there's also the risk that they could harm the child, and it's been stated several times that the SCP is cold, but it isn't cruel. If they can coax the child out instead of simply destroying the blanket and killing the child, then washing their hands of it, I feel they'd take that route. Furthermore, the blanket itself could be quite useful to the SCP, as if something went wrong, it could in fact serve as a line of defense for people who could inhabit it like a bunker. Certainly, if someone finds out about the password, they can enter the world as well, but you DID say you also wanted a weakness to keep it from being too omnipotent. Nevertheless, the SCP could certainly find this blanket useful, in my opinion.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't remember mentioning/recommending the Foundation try to destroy it, which part of the critique(s) gave that impression? apologies in advance if I wasn't clear.</p>
<p>My two major concerns was the blanket having too many effects, and no clear purpose as to why.- (what was the grandmothers reason for making the blanket? why did she feel her child needs this? was he in some sort of danger?)</p>
<p>As for the second major concern; the narrative had no clear arc, and the Foundation didn't seem to have a clear motive, (at least from when it was first described).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>That being said, this blanket DOES only have one major effect now - it creates a &quot;play world&quot; for a child to inhabit, while keeping them safe as they're playing. It's just that the narrative has the child turn that playhouse into a refuge, bracing himself for a siege. The narrative would involve a report of teachers noting that one of their students has been missing for days, and when people came to visit, they found the grandmother deceased and the child missing, though the blanket seemed to be giving off a strange glow. Once pictures of the blanket got to (admittedly at this point, disinterested) SCP personnel, they'd recognize the symbols on the blanket as thaumaturgical and investigate it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This seems much clearer, that said; why was the child's first instinct to run, hide and prepare for siege. What caused the child to feel so threatened/scared?</p>
<p>As it stands;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Once they get a therapist in to help the child get over his grief over his grandmother's passing</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This doesn't really sound like something a child would prepare for a war over? I can definitely understand him not wanting to leave and/or creating barriers to stop people from taking him away (especially if he becomes aware people are actively trying to take him away), however off the bat, preparing for an attack doesn't sound like something a child would do (at least not immediately), if there's no imminent threat.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Upon finding the grandmother's diary, it would be revealed how the blanket works, and after some guesswork, they do manage to figure out the boy's password and send a D-class into the world (not yet realizing that the boy is inhabiting the world). There, the D-class would be attacked by one of the Four Guardians and killed. A second person would be sent in, armed with a firearm, whereupon they'd learn that it doesn't work.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I am skeptical on why the Foundation would decide to send more people in after someone already died, why would they waste resources on something that they already know is actively dangerous? Along with this, trusting a D-class with a gun sounds kinda risky, even if they are 'trustworthy', there's nothing stopping them from firing the weapon off at personnel if they don't want to do something, or feel there's no other option.</p>
<p>quick note: if guns can't work here (presumably for safety), then how/why are people able to die/be killed?- this feels bit contradictory.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This D-class would manage to make it to a town, where the townspeople would helpfully inform them that the &quot;king&quot; has been in the castle for days and refuses to come out.Since the child is referred to as the &quot;king&quot; when the grandmother discusses the world in her diary (meant as a record for her own children and grandchildren if they want to replicate her thaumaturgical endeavors), they realize that if they can get the child out of the world, the blanket will be rendered inert for easy storage.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>more of a nitpick: if he was preparing for a siege, wouldn't he also try to protect his people/the town too? why did he leave them out in the open if he felt threatened?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Again, the narrative helps emphasize that helping the kid out IS the path of least resistance in this case. Once they get a therapist in to help the child get over his grief over his grandmother's passing - the reason he retreated into the world in the first place - they manage to coax him out and give him a dose of amnestics before securing the blanket.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Overall this narrative plan sounds solid to me, the central conflict/emotional investment doesn't feel as forced, and you seem to have a clear idea on what you want to convey. That said, I feel for this to work you will need to show the child and his thought process more, along with the Foundations attempts to coax him out in order to store the skip away. Currently, the child doesn't really have enough of a presence (narrative wise), which makes it hard for me to judge what the draft will look like. As the main 'meat' of the narrative will be focused on the child and how he overcomes his grief.- whats the process behind that?</p>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p>While she did make it so that guns don't work inside the world, she did create weapons that exist inside the world - a magic sword, a fireball staff, that sort of thing - so that if the child wanted to play hero, he could fight with the Four Guardians and &quot;kill&quot; them; the Guardians would allow this, since they're meant to be playmates as much as protectors. Now, this can go one of two ways. We can keep it completely child-friendly, and say that a &quot;dead&quot; person is just expelled from the play-world completely unharmed, which would keep with the theme of the blanket gently protecting the child without hurting him. Or, if we wanted to go the more gruesome route, we could say the kid picked up just a touch of thaumaturgy and found a way to disable the safeties in the world. Frankly, thinking about it now, I kind of like the former, as it keeps more in line with the theme of the blanket being a gentle protector.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My main issue here is the grandmothers motives, there still doesn't seem to be any real reason she made this for him to begin with. There also isn't much on how she may have learnt how to make such an object.</p>
<p>- this doesn't need to be anything super detailed, however without any context, the skip feels unfinished and a little cliched to me, as nothing is really is adding onto the 'security blanket' trope to me.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>So, yes, the main narrative would focus on a few people diving into the world to get him out. The child would still be wrapped up in his grief, but deign to address them from his castle, where his voice echoes from the heavens in the world, like the voice of god. He'd first scream at them to get out, then show how scared and lonely he is but still want to be left alone, and then when the therapist comes in, talk out his grief and problems with them as he/she goes along. Each one, though, would have to initially impress him by making their way deeper into his world, RPG style, since it shows their willingness to play with him.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How do you plan to show this? there isn't much that really gives me an idea on what will actually take place in the narrative, how will this all fit into a draft? what are the child's thoughts on this? how does his relationship with personnel change over time? what is his arc as a character?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>So, the grandmother's motives were nothing more than giving her grandson a fun world to play in. Basically, it was meant to be an elaborate treehouse, or video game for him to play around in. She was a student of thaumaturgy - the townspeople think she's a witch. I mean, is there really a need for the world more than that? I think its simplicity even lends it to the narrative. It's the last remnant this kid has of his grandmother's love for her, and her desire to see him happy.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>The child's attitude would slowly change through his dialogue with the personnel that enter the world, particularly with the therapist talking him through it. Short of typing up all the dialogue, there's not really any way I can convey that in a basic outline post. And his arc would be slowly breaking out of his shell, talking to the personnel more, and accepting his loss.</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
Aight, I think most of this will be dependant on the narrative execution and dialogue. From what I've seen, this works concept wise. So long as there is some context as to why the blanket was created, (which you have shown).
<p>I'll give this a tentative <span style="color: green">Greenlight</span></p>

							</div>

								
